#: skills/workflows/code-quality-gates.bpmn:13
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "FIVE INDEPENDENT JOBS, AND NOTHING IN THE YAML SAYS SO IN ONE PLACE. Bean `7yvd`. The file is 686 lines and reads as a long sequence; it is not one. No job declares `needs:`, so all five start together and the wall-clock cost of the whole workflow is the SLOWEST job, not the sum. That is the single most useful thing to know before adding a gate, and it is recoverable today only by checking five `needs:` keys that are not there.&#10;&#10;THE ASYMMETRY IS THE OTHER HALF. Four jobs are HARD and one, `rust-wildcard`, carries `continue-on-error: true` &#8212; it reports and cannot block. Drawing them as five equal boxes would be a lie a reader would act on, so the warn-only job is labelled as such and the gateway after the join asks specifically about the HARD ones.&#10;&#10;`continue-on-error` IS NOT A DEFAULT HERE, it is a decision that has been REVERSED once already: `python-imports` carried it, and the flag swallowed a failure on a missing path, so the job reported success over a check that never ran. It was removed and a new unused import now fails. The flag survives on exactly one job, and anyone adding it to a second should read that history first.&#10;&#10;`TypeScript` IS ONE BOX STANDING FOR ROUGHLY THIRTY REPOSITORY GATES &#8212; workflow policy, AGENTS.md cross-references and claims, declared assets, workflow skill refs and BPMN coverage, the knowledge-graph audit, generated-docs currency, agent memory, and the ratchet that fails a check script no workflow runs. They are sequential only because one job runs them; each is independent, and `bun run gates` is the local runner derived from this job so a contributor can run the same set before pushing.&#10;&#10;Mechanical throughout. Nothing here waits on a person, which is what makes `build-pipeline` the right lane rather than a convenient one."
msgstr ""

#: skills/workflows/code-quality-gates.bpmn:25
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Joins all five jobs before asking anything, then GW_Hard asks specifically about the four HARD ones — Rust's warn-only result reaches the same join but never the gate, so this lane's merge/block call is deliberately blind to a red job it also ran, which is where the five-are-not-four asymmetry actually bites."
msgstr ""

